    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-araj"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-araj.jpg" alt="Ahmad al-Araj" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-araj">Ahmad al-Araj</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmed_of_Mauretania_(dell%27Altissimi).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cristofano dell'Altissimo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadis</a> were a family from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draa-river">Draa</a> valley in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> who claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and therefore the status of sharifs. In the early sixteenth century Morocco was divided: the Wattasid sultans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> controlled only the north, local lords and religious brotherhoods held much of the rest, and the Portuguese had seized a chain of Atlantic ports including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-morocco">Safi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azemmour">Azemmour</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a>, then known as Santa Cruz do Cabo de Gué. In 1510 the tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sus</a> proclaimed the Saadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-qaim">Muhammad al-Qa’im bi-amr Allah</a> as leader of the holy war against the Portuguese, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jazuliyya">Jazuliyya</a> tradition. His sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-araj">Ahmad al-A’raj</a> and Muhammad al-Shaykh extended the movement northwards and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, probably in 1524 or 1525. Muhammad al-Shaykh captured Agadir in 1541, after which the Portuguese abandoned Safi and Azemmour, and the prestige of this victory allowed him to displace his brother. He then turned on the Wattasids, taking Fez in 1549. The last Wattasid, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abu-hassun">Abu Hassun</a>, retook the city in 1554 with Ottoman troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, but Muhammad al-Shaykh defeated and killed him in the same year, and the Saadis became the undisputed rulers of Morocco from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marrakesh.jpg" alt="Marrakesh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pavillon_Menarag%C3%A4rten.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Acp, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadis</a> remained a largely rural society of farmers and pastoralists, organised in Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> as its great cities. Religious authority was widely shared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods and saintly lineages, whose lodges, or <em>zawiyas</em>, were centres of learning, charity and local power; the Saadis themselves owed their rise to this world. The most dynamic part of the economy was the production of cane <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sus</a> and around Marrakesh, in large mills and estates under royal control, and sugar was exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Moroccan saltpetre, needed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, was traded for English cloth and weapons. After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> in 1591, gold from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> valley arrived in Marrakesh, earning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> the name <em>al-Dhahabi</em>, the Golden, though the flow fell short of his hopes within a few years. Jewish communities played a major role in trade and finance, and the arrival of Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a> expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, culminating in the expulsions of 1609–1614, brought craftsmen, soldiers and corsairs to the Moroccan towns. Plague struck repeatedly, notably in the epidemic of the late 1590s and early 1600s that killed al-Mansur himself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir.jpg" alt="Battle of Alcácer Quibir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lagos46_kopie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georges Jansoone, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Saadi state was born in war, first against the Portuguese enclaves and then against the Wattasids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. Its rulers built an army that combined tribal cavalry with professional troops armed with firearms, including Andalusi refugees, Turkish soldiers and European renegades. Muhammad al-Shaykh drove the Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a> in 1541 and fought off Ottoman ambitions; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-ghalib">Abdallah al-Ghalib</a> defeated an army from Algiers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wadi-al-laban">Wadi al-Laban</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1558. The dynasty’s most famous victory came on 4 August 1578 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a>, also called the Battle of Wadi al-Makhazin or the Battle of the Three Kings. The deposed sultan Muhammad al-Mutawakkil had enlisted King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian of Portugal</a> against his uncle Abd al-Malik, who had taken the throne in 1576 with Ottoman help. The Portuguese army was destroyed, and Sebastian, al-Mutawakkil and Abd al-Malik, who died of illness during the battle, all perished. In 1590 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> sent an expedition of a few thousand men, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>, under the Spanish-born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judar-pasha">Judar Pasha</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>; it defeated the much larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a> in March 1591 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, creating the Pashalik of Timbuktu.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur.jpg" alt="Ahmad al-Mansur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmad_al-Mansur_by_Andr%C3%A9_Thevet.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">André Thevet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saadi legitimacy rested on sharifian descent and leadership in the holy war, and the dynasty presented its rulers as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> in their own right, refusing to acknowledge the Ottoman sultan. Government was centred on the <em>makhzen</em>, the royal household and administration, supported by tribes that supplied soldiers in return for tax exemptions, a system later inherited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouites</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> (r. 1578–1603) gave the court a highly formal ceremonial, partly modelled on Ottoman practice, organised the army along Ottoman lines and kept a large chancery that produced diplomatic and literary works. He used the ransoms of Portuguese nobles captured in 1578 to fill the treasury and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-badi-palace">El Badi</a> palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>. In foreign policy he balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and opened relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>: he exchanged letters with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, and in 1600 his envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-wahid-ben-messaoud">Abd al-Wahid ben Messaoud</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to propose an alliance against Spain. The sultans relied on chief ministers, provincial governors and qa’ids, and on the cooperation of religious brotherhoods, though their attempts to control independent religious leaders created lasting tensions. Succession was not regulated, and the competition of brothers and sons for the throne was a weakness that became fatal after 1603.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saadian-tombs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saadian-tombs.jpg" alt="Saadian Tombs" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saadian-tombs">Saadian Tombs</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saadian_Tombs_1044-HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">C messier, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadis</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> a state that resisted both Iberian conquest and Ottoman domination, so that it alone of the North African states never became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Their victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Alcácer Quibir</a> had consequences far beyond Morocco: the death of the childless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian</a> led to a succession crisis that ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> taking the Portuguese crown in 1580, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberian-union">Iberian Union</a>. The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> in 1591 ended the last great empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger bend</a> and left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Arma</a>, the descendants of the Moroccan soldiers, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> long after Saadi control had ended. The dynasty established the principle that the ruler of Morocco should be a sharif, a principle continued by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouite dynasty</a>, which still reigns today. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> the Saadis left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadian-tombs">Saadian Tombs</a>, the remains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-badi-palace">El Badi</a> palace and the Ben Youssef <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>, rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-ghalib">Abdallah al-Ghalib</a> in the 1560s. Their court supported scholarship and poetry, and historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-al-fishtali">Abd al-Aziz al-Fishtali</a> glorified al-Mansur’s reign. The dealings of al-Mansur with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> were part of the early history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>’s relations with the Islamic world, and his envoy’s visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> has been linked to the context of Shakespeare’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/othello">Othello</a></em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco.jpg" alt="Al-Rashid of Morocco" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco">Al-Rashid of Morocco</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cherif_Muley-Arxid.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolas de Larmessin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> from plague in 1603 unleashed a civil war among his sons Zidan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-faris-abd-al-aziz-ii">Abu Faris</a> and Muhammad al-Shaykh al-Ma’mun. The kingdom split between rival rulers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, and in 1610 al-Ma’mun handed the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larache">Larache</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in return for support, a concession that discredited the dynasty. Zidan held Marrakesh but lost much of his authority; in 1612 his library was seized at sea by Spanish ships and ended up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">Escorial</a>. Effective control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> valley lapsed, and the pashas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> became independent in practice by the 1610s. Across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> regional powers filled the vacuum: the marabout al-Ayyashi waged his own war against the Spanish enclaves, the Sharifate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tazeroualt">Tazeroualt</a> ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sus</a>, the Dila’iyya brotherhood came to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-atlas">Middle Atlas</a> and Fez, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Morisco</a> corsairs founded the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> in 1627. By the 1640s the Saadi sultans held little more than Marrakesh. The last of them, Ahmad al-Abbas, was killed in 1659 by the family of his mother, and the city passed to local leaders. Within a few years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouites</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a>, another sharifian family, reunited the country: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco">Mulay al-Rashid</a> took Fez in 1666 and Marrakesh soon afterwards.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Saadi Sultanate reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> under a sharifian dynasty, expelled the Portuguese from much of its coast, kept the country outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a> and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>, establishing the model of sharifian monarchy that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouites</a> still follow.</em></p>

